{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>Few and evil have been the days of the years of my life.</b> I do not know the reason of our father the elder — what rebuke is this, that he should complain to the king, and what reason is there to say, 'and they have not attained the days of the years of the lives of my fathers,' for perhaps he will yet attain them and live longer than they. And it appears to me that our father Jacob — hoary age had come upon him and he appeared very old, and Pharaoh was astonished at his old age, for most people of his time do not live so long, since their years have already grown short. Therefore he asked him, 'How many are the days of the years of your life?' — for 'I have not seen anyone as old as you in all my kingdom.' Then Jacob answered that his days are one hundred and thirty years, and he should not be astonished by them, for they are few compared to the years of his fathers who lived longer; but because they were evil with toil and sighing, hoary age had come upon him and he appeared very old."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 47, :notes {0 "I read 'הזקן אבינו' as an epithet for Jacob; it could alternatively be read as 'our grandfather,' but the context and the subsequent 'יעקב אבינו' support the identification with Jacob."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:22:29.511055989Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}